  :adr/triage
  [{:construct "map + keyword"
    :usage-frequency "13-15/17 sampled libs, core (chobo/mise/spec data model)"
    :safety-impact "orthogonal to T1/T2/T3; existing heap-pair desugar technique
                     (already used for not/zero?/pos?/neg? in compiler/) extends directly"
    :verdict :add :priority :highest}
   {:construct "destructuring ({:keys []}, [a b & rest])"
    :usage-frequency "14/17, core"
    :safety-impact "pure syntax sugar over map/vector access, no new runtime capability"
    :verdict :add :priority :after-map-keyword}
   {:construct "and/or/when in compiler/"
    :usage-frequency "n/a (kotoba/ already supports these safely)"
    :safety-impact "zero -- kotoba/'s working implementation is the safety proof"
    :verdict :add :priority :low-cost-port}
   {:construct "vector-as-data"
    :usage-frequency "10/17, mostly ordered tuples"
    :safety-impact "same heap-pair desugar approach as maps"
    :verdict :add :priority :medium}
   {:construct "multi-arity defn"
    :usage-frequency "13/17, core (default-argument pattern)"
    :safety-impact "each arity compiles to a statically-known separate function"
    :verdict :add :priority :medium}
   {:construct "loop/recur"
    :usage-frequency "7/17"
    :safety-impact "kotoba/ already documents unbounded self-recursion StackOverflow
                     as a known risk; loop compiles to a WASM loop instruction with
                     no call-stack growth -- a SAFETY IMPROVEMENT, not just a feature"
    :verdict :add :priority :safety-positive}
   {:construct "general first-class functions / HOF (map/reduce with a passed lambda)"
    :usage-frequency "15/17, core, BUT"
    :safety-impact "infer-effects is a static fixpoint over KNOWN call sites; an
                     arbitrary closure argument could carry an undeclared effect
                     invisible to the analysis -- breaks T2 soundness, not just a
                     missing feature"
    :verdict :do-not-add-general-form
    :alternative "bounded map/reduce accepting only references to known top-level defns,
                  same design as kotoba/'s existing call-indirect (funcref table)"}
   {:construct "defprotocol/defrecord/multimethod"
    :usage-frequency "3/17 (mise/chobo strategy pattern), defmulti/defmethod 0/17"
    :safety-impact "type-based dynamic dispatch has the same soundness problem as general HOF"
    :verdict :do-not-add
    :alternative "rewrite the 3 affected libraries to keyword-tagged data + case
                  dispatch once map/keyword lands -- closed dispatch sets are also
                  more in the spirit of safe-Kotoba's deny-by-default design than
                  open protocol extension"}
   {:construct "atom/ref/volatile!/set! (guest-side general mutable state)"
    :usage-frequency "~1 genuinely load-bearing persistent-state use per library
                       (registry/checkpoint/db-connection); host-side metering atoms
                       (aiueos.execute/kototama.tender) are a DIFFERENT category"
    :safety-impact "guest mutable state breaks the effect-fixpoint's referential-
                     transparency assumption; host-side atoms IMPLEMENT the
                     confinement boundary rather than eroding it"
    :verdict :do-not-add-for-guest :host-side-unchanged true}
   {:construct "try/catch/throw (guest-side)"
    :usage-frequency "heavy, split between ordinary error reporting and
                       security-relevant abort paths (aiueos.execute's ex-data
                       vocabulary for quota/fuel/capability-denial)"
    :safety-impact "a guest catching a quota/fuel-exceeded exception could swallow
                     it and keep running, defeating resource limits"
    :verdict :do-not-add}
   {:construct "defmacro / runtime require/resolve / reflection"
    :usage-frequency "near-zero (0 dynamic-require hits across 15 repos, 1 trivial
                       sugar-only macro in the whole sample)"
    :safety-impact "classic static-check-bypass attack surface"
    :verdict :do-not-add}]
  :adr/langchain-db-kotobase-integration
  {:current-state "langchain.db/create-conn returns a bare (atom {:db ... :log []}),
                    process-memory-only. Its own docstring already documents intent
                    to swap in a real Datomic Local / DataScript backend via the
                    langchain.db/api map."
   :kotobase-api
   {:protocol "kotobase.store/IStore: -put/-get/-list (doc space, last-writer-wins),
               -append/-read (append-only stream, monotonic :seq cursor)"
    :implementations ["kotobase.local/LocalStore (pure atom, OSS standalone + reference oracle)"
                       "kotobase.kotobase/KotobaseStore (forwards to injected xrpc fn -> kotobase.net PDS)"]
    :precedent "kotobase.store's own docstring: same injection pattern as the
                actors' MemStore/DatomicStore and num-clj's IBackend -- this
                exact swap is already an established ecosystem convention"}
   :design-decision
   ["langchain.db does NOT hard-depend on kotobase.store -- preserves ADR-0001's
     zero-third-party-runtime-deps promise for the main :deps"
    "create-conn gains an optional duck-typed persist map: {:append (fn [event] ...)
     :read (fn [since] -> [events])} -- same method shapes as IStore's -append/-read,
     no protocol :require needed in langchain.db itself"
    "on conn creation with persist supplied: replay ((:read persist) 0) through the
     existing pure `with` tx-application logic to rebuild the in-memory :db value"
    "transact! calls ((:append persist) {:tx ... :tx-data ...}) exactly once, AFTER
     the swap! resolves (using the winning @report), never inside the swap! fn body
     -- avoids duplicate appends under CAS-retry contention (the existing transact!
     docstring already documents a real concurrency bug this exact pattern must avoid)"
    "q/pull/entid/db/datoms/api map: UNCHANGED -- langchain.memory and
     langgraph.checkpoint (consumers of :db-api) are unaffected"
    "the actual kotobase wiring lives in a NEW optional namespace langchain.kotobase-db
     (:require kotobase.store), added to a new :kotobase alias's :extra-deps in
     langchain/deps.edn, NOT the main :deps -- same isolation pattern langchain.jvm
     already uses for http-kit/jsonista"]
   :status "implemented and landed same session -- see :adr/implementation-2026-07-14"}
  :adr/implementation-2026-07-14
  {:summary "Implemented as designed with 2 deviations from the original plan,
             recorded honestly: (1) the adapter namespace ended up named
             langchain.kotobase-persist, not langchain.kotobase-db (that name
             was already taken by a pre-existing, unrelated remote-XRPC adapter
             discovered during implementation); (2) kotobase was added to the
             EXISTING :test alias's :extra-deps rather than a new :kotobase
             alias -- simpler and matches the http-kit/jsonista precedent
             directly."
   :forward-reference-bug
   "create-conn's new persist-replay branch calls `with`, defined later in the
    file -- clojure -M:test failed to compile until (declare with) was added
    right after the ns form. Caught by actually running the test suite, not
    just by design review."
   :known-limitation-not-fixed
   "concurrent transact! calls on the same conn can append to persist in a
    different order than their tx numbers were assigned in-memory (each call's
    -append happens in a separate step after its own swap! resolves, not
    atomically with tx-number assignment) -- documented as a limitation in
    transact!'s docstring, not fixed in this pass"
   :discovered-during-implementation
   "langchain already has langchain.kotoba-db: a SEPARATE, pre-existing full
    Datomic-shaped langchain.db/api implementation talking directly to a
    kotoba-server's ai.gftd.apps.kotobase.datomic.* XRPC endpoints (thin
    client, no local query engine at all). Does not conflict with this ADR's
    integration (which instead persists langchain.db's own local Datalog
    engine's tx log via kotobase.store/IStore) -- the two are complementary,
    different architecture tradeoffs, both remain valid."
   :verification
